class Meta(type):
    """A metaclass is a class whose instances are classes."""
    def __new__(cls, name, bases, attrs):
        # Custom initialization logic can be added here
        print(f"Creating class {name}")
        return super().__new__(cls, name, bases, attrs)

class MyClass(metaclass=Meta):
    """An example of a class created with a custom metaclass."""
    pass

print(MyClass)
```

This code defines a metaclass `Meta` and uses it to create a new class `MyClass`. The `__new__` method of the metaclass is overridden to include custom initialization logic. When `MyClass` is instantiated, this method is called, printing "Creating class MyClass". This demonstrates how metaclasses can customize the creation process of classes in Python.

