Title: What does O(log n) mean exactly?.
Body: <p>I am learning about Big O Notation running times and amortized times.  I understand the notion of <em>O(n)</em> linear time, meaning that the size of the input affects the growth of the algorithm proportionally...and the same goes for, for example, quadratic time <em>O(n<sup>2</sup>)</em> etc..even algorithms, such as permutation generators, with <em>O(n!)</em> times, that grow by factorials.</p>

<p>For example, the following function is <em>O(n)</em> because the algorithm grows in proportion to its input <em>n</em>:</p>

<pre><code>f(int n) {
  int i;
  for (i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i)
    printf("%d", i);
}
</code></pre>

<p>Similarly, if there was a nested loop, the time would be O(n<sup>2</sup>).</p>

<p>But what exactly is <em>O(log n)</em>?  For example, what does it mean to say that the height of a complete binary tree is <em>O(log n)</em>?</p>

<p>I do know (maybe not in great detail) what Logarithm is, in the sense that:  log<sub>10</sub> 100 = 2, but I cannot understand how to identify a function with a logarithmic time.</p>

answer:
```python
class MyClass:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value

    def __hash__(self):
        # A simple hash function that combines the hash values of all attributes
        return hash((id(self), self.value))
```

In this solution, we define a class `MyClass` with an attribute `value`. We override the `__hash__` method to provide a custom implementation. The hash function uses the identity (`id`) of the object and the value itself as components of the hash. This approach ensures that objects with the same `value` but different identities will have the same hash, which can be useful for collections like dictionaries where uniqueness of keys is crucial.

This example demonstrates a basic strategy for creating a hash function that incorporates both the object's identity and its content. In more complex scenarios, you might need to consider other factors such as the attributes of the object, the data type of the attribute, or even use a more sophisticated hashing algorithm.

def calculate_time_complexity(code):
    # Identify and count operations
    operations = []

    # Parse the code and extract operations
    lines = code.split('\n')
    for line in lines:
        if 'for' in line:
            operations.append((line.strip(), 'loop'))
        elif 'if' in line:
            operations.append((line.strip(), 'if'))
        elif '=' in line:
            operations.append((line.strip(), 'assignment'))

    # Sum up the operations
    total_operations = len(operations)

    # Express in Big O notation
    big_o_notation = f'O({total_operations})'

    return big_o_notation

